course: dictionary
lesson: 1492
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. «Never go into old mines, caves or tunnels» uses cave as a place, a large hole in a hill, cliff or under the ground. Read the words around it to find the meaning.
explain: Cave can also be a verb. «The roof began to cave in after the heavy snow» shows that cave in means to fall down inwards, like a roof. The word in is a clue, because it tells you the roof went down, not up.
explain: Cave in can also mean to stop arguing and agree because of pressure. If someone says, «After an hour of arguing, Sam finally caved in and agreed,» the pressure of the argument has made him give up. Ask yourself: what is happening to the people in the sentence?
fact: A cave is a large hole in the side of a hill or cliff, or under the ground.
fact: «Cave in» means to fall down inwards, like a roof.
fact: «Cave in» can also mean to stop arguing and agree because of pressure.
fact: Caves form when water slowly dissolves rock such as limestone; stalactites hang down from the roof and stalagmites grow up from the floor.
fact: «Cave paintings» in places such as Lascaux in France show horses, bulls and deer, and some are over 30,000 years old.
exercise: «Never go into old mines, caves or tunnels.» What do caves, mines and tunnels have in common here? | *They are holes under the ground | They are always full of water | They are all in towns and cities | They are all made of glass
exercise: «Waves cut caves into headlands.» What cuts the caves? | Snow | Bats | *Waves | Children
exercise: «The roof began to cave in after the heavy snow.» What does cave in mean here? | Stop arguing and agree | *Fall down inwards | Paint pictures on the walls | Climb up to the top
test: «Bats sleep hanging upside down, and bats are not blind.» What does this sentence tell us? | Bats never sleep | Bats cannot fly | Bats live only in trees | *Bats are not blind
test: After an argument, Sam «caved in» and agreed with his sister. What does cave in mean here? | *Stop arguing and agree because of pressure | Dig a deep hole | Shout louder than before | Fall down like a roof
exam: Which sentence uses cave as a noun, meaning a hole? | «Please do not cave under the pressure.» | *«The explorers climbed down into the dark cave.» | «They finally caved in and agreed to our plan.» | «The old roof began to cave in.»
